# Open Robot Mower: a DIY robot lawn mower, fully designed and open source

An open-source robotic lawn mower you can build from laser-cut aluminium,
3D-printed parts and parts you can buy:

- **Wire-free.** It navigates by RTK GNSS to a couple of centimetres, so
  there is no boundary wire to bury. A perimeter-wire option is described
  for lawns under heavy tree cover.
- **Guarded blade.** A 200 mm disc with three pivoting razor blades cuts a
  232 mm swath at 20-50 mm, inside a full guard ring that reaches below the
  blade plane.
- **Floating shell.** The shell sits on four rubber mounts with 3-axis hall
  sensors: lift it or bump it and the blade stops.
- **Safety firmware.** An ESP32 owns the motors and every safety input. The
  blade runs only while the mower is level, the shell is down, it is dry,
  the stop button is up and the planner's heartbeat is fresh.
- **Docks to charge** on a pegged-down dock with contacts that stay dead
  until a mower is on them.

**Plan your own lawn in your browser:**
https://duncan-haywood.github.io/oss-monorepo/open-robot-mower/

Everything is MIT-licensed. You can build it, change it, and sell it under
your own brand. If you sell it, you are the manufacturer (see
[safety](hardware/safety.md)).

> **Status: design v0.1, unbuilt, simulation-tested.** The CAD passes its
> collision, clearance, hole-alignment and printer-fit checks, and the
> planner, simulation and firmware logic are unit-tested. No mower has been
> built yet, and every performance number below is a model under stated
> assumptions. Builders: please open issues with what you find.

## Manufacture it

A parametric CAD model in code ([`cad/`](cad/README.md), build123d)
generates the whole fab package. It is committed at
[`manufacturing/mower-package.zip`](manufacturing/mower-package.zip)
([browse](manufacturing/mower/); GitHub renders the STLs in 3D), with the
dock at [`manufacturing/dock-package.zip`](manufacturing/dock-package.zip):

- `laser/*.dxf`: chassis, height plate, electronics tray and blade disc,
  flat 5052 aluminium, no bends or tapped holes
- `print/*.stl`: 16 ASA parts (shell in six segments, guard quarters,
  motor clamps, wheel hubs, caster forks, shell posts, height gauge...),
  each fitting a 256 mm printer bed
- `step/*.step`: every part, plus the assembly
- `bom.csv` (every part and fastener), `cut_list.csv` and assembly drawings

Every build checks: design rules (guard clearance and depth, blade distance
from the shell edge, motor and caster fit); pairwise collisions with the
spinning parts given 2 mm running clearance, **at all seven cut heights**;
the shell pushed 8 mm each way without touching anything; wheels and
casters on the ground; every printed part on the printer bed; and, for the
dock, the default mower docked without touching anything but the contacts.
Builds are byte-reproducible, and CI fails if the committed package goes
stale.

## What's here

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| [`hardware/mower.md`](hardware/mower.md) | The mower: chassis, drive, cutting system, height adjustment, guard, shell and sensors |
| [`hardware/navigation.md`](hardware/navigation.md) | RTK base and rover, mapping a lawn, fix losses, the perimeter-wire alternative |
| [`hardware/dock.md`](hardware/dock.md) | Charging dock and its dead-until-docked contacts |
| [`hardware/electronics.md`](hardware/electronics.md) | Block diagram, ESP32 pin map, power budget, wiring |
| [`hardware/safety.md`](hardware/safety.md) | The hazard in numbers, what the design does and does not protect against, rules for running it |
| [`hardware/bom-*.csv`](hardware/) | Bills of materials (rough estimates: ~$1,050 mower and dock, ~$300 RTK base) |
| [`src/mowerbot/`](src/mowerbot/) | Python package for the Pi: lawn geometry, coverage planner, coverage raster, line follower, closed-loop simulation, power and charge sessions, blade stop time, slope limits, serial protocol |
| [`firmware/esp32/`](firmware/esp32/README.md) | MicroPython firmware: blade interlock, latched stops, watchdog, soft start, blade-stop check |
| [`cad/`](cad/README.md), [`manufacturing/`](manufacturing/) | Parametric CAD (build123d) and the generated fab packages |
| `index.html`, `sim.mjs` | The browser lawn planner (a port of the Python planner, tested against it) |

## Quick start (software)

```
pip install "git+https://github.com/duncan-haywood/oss-monorepo#subdirectory=ventures/open-robot-mower"

mowerbot plan --example backyard              # stripes, loops, time, planned coverage
mowerbot plan --lawn lawn.example.json --angle 30 --path-out path.json
mowerbot simulate --runs 3                    # drive it with RTK noise, wheel slip and fix losses
mowerbot schedule --battery-wh 40             # split a lawn into charge sessions
mowerbot runtime --slope 10                   # power budget and runtime per charge
mowerbot blade-stop --phase-r 0.6             # tip speed, stored energy, stop time
mowerbot slope                                # steepest workable slope, dry and wet
```

Coordinates are metres in a local east-north frame (the RTK base station
is a natural origin). A lawn file is
`{"outer": [[x, y], ...], "holes": [[[x, y], ...]], "dock": [x, y]}`; the
browser planner exports one.

## What the models say about the default design

All of these are outputs of this repository's code on the default
parameters, not measurements.

| Question | Answer | Command |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Back-yard example (186 m², a bed and a tree): plan | 109 stripes at 170°, 979 m, 52 min, 99.96% planned coverage | `mowerbot plan` |
| Same lawn, closed loop (1.5 cm RTK noise, 3% slip, 2 fix losses/h) | 99.86-99.88% cut, 55-57 min, worst cross-track 6.5 cm | `mowerbot simulate --runs 3` |
| Power while cutting at 0.35 m/s on the flat | 32.7 W (drive 7.1, blade 18.6, electronics 7.0), ~3.1 h on 126 Wh | `mowerbot runtime` |
| Blade stop from 2,800 rpm | 0.46 s short-braked, 13.7 s coasting | `mowerbot blade-stop` |
| Blade tip speed, stored energy | 34 m/s, 51 J (1.4 J in each blade) | `mowerbot blade-stop` |
| Steepest workable slope | 16.7° dry, 5.1° wet, limited by climbing traction | `mowerbot slope` |

Read it as:

- **Coverage is not the problem; traction is.** The planner and follower
  leave well under 1% of a lawn uncut in simulation, mostly in sharp inside
  corners. The weak point is rear drive on wet grass: about 5 degrees. Keep
  weight over the drive wheels, and do not mow wet.
- **The brake matters more than anything else for safety.** Coasting, the
  disc spins for about 14 s; shorted phases stop it in about half a second.
  That is why the stop button drops a relay whose normally-closed contacts
  short the motor, independent of the ESC and the firmware.
- **Every coefficient is an assumption** (rolling resistance, cutting energy,
  grass friction, motor constants). The first build should measure them and
  feed them back in through the CLI options.

## How the pieces fit

```
RTK base ──RTCM──> RTK rover ──fix──┐
                                    v
lawn.json ──> planner.plan ──> path ──> drive.LineFollower ──> protocol DRIVE / BLADE ──UART──> ESP32
                  │                                                                          │
                  └─> power.schedule (go home to charge)          controller.py: blade interlock, latched stops,
                                                                  watchdog, soft start, stop-time check
```

## Develop

```
cd ventures/open-robot-mower
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m unittest discover -s tests   # planner, coverage, follower, simulation, physics, firmware
node --test *.test.mjs                                 # browser port vs Python fixtures, page checks, usage counter
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m mowerbot.fixtures            # regenerate fixtures after changing the planner
(cd cad && pip install -r requirements.txt && python3 -m unittest discover -s tests)   # CAD checks, ~2 min
```

## Roadmap (long-term project)

1. **Build the chassis and drive first**, blade disc off: test the RTK
   fix, mapping and the line follower on a real lawn, and measure rolling
   resistance and slip.
2. Bench-test the blade with the guard on and the mower upside down in a
   cage: measure the real stop time with a slow-motion video.
3. Design the hall-sensor helper board and the dock's charge-enable
   circuit to schematics.
4. Measure power over a real session and replace the assumed coefficients.
5. Add obstacle detection ahead of the mower (ultrasonic or a camera) so it
   does not rely on touch alone.

